+## Frequency lower bound
+
+`PackedLexiconData` derives the minimum terminal rank for each subtree during
+its existing off-main, depth-bounded postorder validation. The stack starts with
+the node's own terminal rank (unknown for nonterminals); completed child minima
+propagate to the parent. The published immutable handle retains only two bytes
+per node. Values 1..65534 are lower bounds, 65535 represents `Int.MAX_VALUE`
+(no ranked terminal). Larger finite ranks round down to 65534, never up; the
+current pinned ranks <=50000 are represented exactly. Structural and rank
+validation and canonical-word hashing still precede publication. Failed or
+cancelled validation cannot publish partially derived bounds.
+
+Every terminal reachable from a frontier region has rank at least its stored
+minimum. Including an already visited own terminal or a policy-ineligible
+descendant only weakens the bound. The comparator's lower bound is therefore
+`(W, -prior, minimumRank, LEX_BOTTOM, ...)`. Streaming uses strict lexicographic
+separation, with no new pruning of unverified terminals or provisional quota
+selection. The complete child-list expansion rule and both shared caps are
+unchanged. Rank metadata contains only public dictionary information and follows
+the same immutable handle lifecycle; no typing payload or new persistence.
+
+The separate CC-BY-SA frequency assets and APK bytes are unchanged. Derived
+arrays cost 544770 bytes EN, 2501958 ES and 4511732 RU (7558460 total), plus
+ordinary array headers. The added frontier rank scratch is 32776 bytes per active
+engine. These capacities must be weighed against measured search and load CPU;
+they do not establish physical memory or battery acceptance.
